    "description": {
      "value": "Greater burdock is a biennial plant, rather tall, reaching as much as 3 metres (10 feet). The fleshy taproot can grow up to 1 m (3+1⁄2 ft) long and 2 centimetres (3⁄4 in) across. It has large, alternating, wavy-edged cordiform leaves that have a long petiole and are pubescent on the underside. The flowers are purple and grouped in globular capitula, united in clusters. They appear in mid-summer, from July to September. The capitula are surrounded by an involucre made out of many bracts, each curving to form a hook, allowing the mature fruits to be carried long distances.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctium_lappa",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-29"
      }
    },
    "distribution": {
      "value": "This species is native to the temperate regions of the Old World, from Scandinavia to the Mediterranean, and from the British Isles through Russia, and the Middle East to India, China, Taiwan and Japan. It is naturalized widely in temperate climates and is usually found in disturbed areas, especially in soil rich in humus and nitrogen, preferring full sunlight.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctium_lappa",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-29"
      }
    },
    "constituents": {
      "value": "Burdock roots contain mucilage, sulfurous acetylene compounds, polyacetylenes and bitter guaianolide-type constituents. Seeds contain arctigenin, arctiin, and butyrolactone lignans.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctium_lappa",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-29"
      }
    }
