    "description": {
      "value": "This herbaceous perennial grows to a height of 1 metre (3+1⁄2 ft) from underground rhizomes. The stems are erect, hollow, and grooved. The upper leaves are ternate, broad and toothed. It flowers in spring and early summer. Numerous flowers are grouped together in an umbrella-shaped flowerhead known as a compound umbel. This is divided into several individual umbels (known as umbellets). Each umbellet has 15 to 20 rays (pedicels) that are each topped with a single, small, five-petaled white flower. These are visited by many types of pollinating insects.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegopodium_podagraria",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-29"
      }
    },
    "distribution": {
      "value": "Aegopodium podagraria is distributed widely in the temperate zone of western Asia and the whole of mainland Europe. It has been introduced elsewhere, including Ireland, the United Kingdom, the United States, Canada, Australia (including Tasmania), New Zealand, and Japan.",
      "source": {
        "name": "Wikipedia",
        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegopodium_podagraria",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-29"
      }
    }
